artifact.self_contained_problem:
Fix an integer $
u \ge 3$ and define the map $T:\mathbb{N}	o\mathbb{N}$ by
$$T(n)= egin{cases} n/2 & 	ext{if } n 	ext{ is even},\ ((2^{
u}-1)n+1)/2 & 	ext{if } n 	ext{ is odd}.\end{cases}$$
For $n\in\mathbb{N}$, let the trajectory of $n$ be the sequence $n, T(n), T^2(n), \dots$. Call a finite sequence $(n_0	o n_1	o\cdots	o n_{k-1}	o n_0)$ a cycle of $T$ if $T(n_i)=n_{i+1 mod k}$ for all $i$, and let $\mathcal{C}$ denote the set of all cycles of $T$ in $\mathbb{N}$. Note that
$$\Omega(1):\quad 1	o 2^{
u}-1	o 2^{
u-2}\cdot	ext{(odd)}	o\cdots	o 1,$$
more precisely the cycle $1	o 2^{
u}-1	o 2^{
u-2}	o 2^{
u-3}	o \cdots 	o 2	o 1$ of length $
u$, is always a cycle of $T$. Let $G(1)=\{n\in\mathbb{N}:	ext{the trajectory of } n 	ext{ eventually enters } \Omega(1)\}$ and $G(\infty)=\{n\in\mathbb{N}:T^k(n)	o\infty 	ext{ as } k	o\infty\}$.

Prove or disprove the following two statements for every integer $
u\ge 3$:
(a) $\mathcal{C}=\{\Omega(1)\}$, i.e. $\Omega(1)$ is the only cycle of $T$ in $\mathbb{N}$.
(b) $\mathbb{N}=G(1)\cup G(\infty)$ with $G(\infty)
e\emptyset$ (so every positive integer either reaches the trivial cycle or has a divergent trajectory, and divergent trajectories exist).

artifact.status_search_result:
This is a conjecture in the family of generalized Collatz / Syracuse problems with multiplier $a=2^{
u}-1$ and additive constant $b=1$. Even the classical case $
u=2$ (the $3n+1$ problem) is famously open, and no resolution is known for the $(2^{
u}-1)n+1$ generalizations for $
u\ge 3$. No published solution was located.
